Doing it your way doesn't take into account the added value of getting
to use the rest of the marker for something else. It's not just about
getting the spell, it's also about having a marker available for
future use.

Expected value means "the expected amount from a given random
scenario". for example, lets say we play a game where you pay me $3.
Then we roll a d6, and I give you the number on the die. You only make
money or break even 66% of the time (not enough for your 80%), but the
game still has an expected value of $.50. In the long run, you will
make money, even if you roll a 1 or a 2 on the first few tries.

> Assuming you are writing a book that takes an average of 15
> charges, that would be about 75 charges to write the book.
>

It's not 75 charges to write the book. Sometimes you get the book
right on the first try. Then you have the whole rest of the marker to
do with what you please. Of course, it could take 750 charges. But
expected value tries to put a good guess on how many charges it will
take. If you wrote a program to simulate book writing, ran it a
million times, averaged them all together, you would get about 47
charges per attempt. I'll take the risk of it taking more for the
chance at the whole rest of the marker.

